{
  // Deploy config for the fids-proxy backend.
  //
  // This did not exist in the repo. fids-proxy has been deployed by hand
  // ("Source: Upload", last deployment 2026-06-20) from a folder that isn't
  // here, so the binding list lived only on the deployer's machine — which is
  // why a security fix to workers/fids-proxy.js could not be shipped from here.
  //
  // Every entry below was derived from two sources that agree:
  //   1. every `env.X` the code actually reads (grepped, complete — no others)
  //   2. the live resources in the Cloudflare account (ids read back from it)
  //
  // SECRETS ARE DELIBERATELY ABSENT. JWT_SECRET, SEED_ADMIN_PASSWORD, ADB_KEY,
  // ADB_WEBHOOK_SECRET, AEROAPI_KEY, ACCOR_KEY, NINJAS_KEY, MCO_FEED_KEY,
  // VECTEEZY_TOKEN and VECTEEZY_ACCOUNT_ID are Worker secrets. They persist
  // across deploys and must never be committed. The two VECTEEZY_* secrets are
  // optional — without them /api/vecteezy/* answers 503 and the rest of the
  // worker is unaffected. (A VECTEEZY_RAPIDAPI_KEY secret may linger from a
  // briefly-used, since-removed fallback route; the code ignores it — see
  // docs/operations/vecteezy-stock.md.)
  "$schema": "../node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "fids-proxy",
  "main": "fids-proxy.js",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-05-27",
  "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat"],

  // Reached at fids-proxy.n-leblanc1984.workers.dev — a workers.dev subdomain,
  // not a custom domain. Keeping this true preserves that hostname.
  "workers_dev": true,

  "kv_namespaces": [
    { "binding": "FIDS_USERS",        "id": "1ed38d7f081a4b58bd2811b12e217050" },
    { "binding": "FIDS_LIVE_FLIGHTS", "id": "23d2a25c9a614f07b7dc860ec4c89e3f" },
    { "binding": "CITY_BG_CACHE",     "id": "9aa58d273cc64dc08992a35b3c508c8f" }
  ],

  "r2_buckets": [
    { "binding": "FIDS_ASSETS", "bucket_name": "fids-assets" }
  ],

  "ai": { "binding": "AI" },

  // 2026-09-04 — ADB QUOTA KILL SWITCH. v23255 put AeroDataBox first in line
  // for every live-position lookup; on gate screens that poll every 60 s,
  // 24/7, against a 40 s cache, that burned through the RapidAPI plan (the
  // quota-exhausted email of 2026-09-04). "community" makes /adsb/* skip ADB
  // entirely and use the free community ring — the pre-v23255 behaviour;
  // fids-proxy.js already honours it (see _adbFirst). Schedule/FIDS window
  // data is unaffected and still uses ADB. Delete this var (or set "adb")
  // only together with real position caching, or the burn resumes.
  // ── FR24 DAILY BUDGET RAISED 2026-09-11, ON THE OWNER'S INSTRUCTION ──
  // Was the code default of 240/day. Measured spend at the time of the change,
  // from FR24's own usage endpoint over 24h:
  //
  //   live/flight-positions/full    552 calls   1476 credits   (2.7 per call)
  //   flight-summary/full            10 calls    587 credits   (59 per call)
  //   total                         562 calls   2063 credits
  //
  // So real demand is already more than double the 240 cap, which is why the
  // budget was being exhausted daily and FR24 going quiet — taking registration,
  // aircraft type and track with it (see v23732).
  //
  // RAISED AGAIN 2026-09-11: an unused allowance is money already spent, so
  // the cap should sit near the ceiling rather than leave half of it idle.
  //
  // 1,800/day is 55,800 in a 31-day month — 93% of the agreed 60,000/month
  // CALL budget, with the remaining 7% as headroom for a busy day. Against the
  // observed 562/day that is better than 3x the room, which is what stops an
  // ordinary day's lookups from ever hitting the gate.
  //
  // THE TWO METERS ARE NOW THE SAME THING. The note that used to sit here was
  // right that they were not: this counter incremented once per HTTP CALL while
  // FR24 bills per RETURNED ROW, so the cap limited request count and not
  // money. That was fixed in #803 (fr24Charge), and the counter now accumulates
  // real credits. The number below therefore changed meaning, and had to be
  // re-chosen rather than inherited.
  //
  // LOWERED 2026-09-13: 1,800 as a CALL cap was fine; as a CREDIT cap it is
  // 55,800 in a 31-day month. That fits the 60,000 the account has until the
  // end of the year and badly overshoots the 30,000 it drops back to in
  // January — the published Explorer allowance, with the extra being an Early
  // Access promotion that does not survive renewal. Credits do not roll over,
  // so there is nothing banked to soften that.
  //
  // 950/day is 29,450 in a 31-day month: inside 30,000 permanently. One value
  // that is correct before and after the change, so nothing has to be
  // remembered at year end and no board quietly goes dark in January.
  //
  // Headroom check against real demand: measured burn was ~3,000 credits/day,
  // roughly 80% of it spent re-asking FR24 every 30 seconds whether a parked
  // aeroplane had started transmitting. The caching fix in this same change
  // cuts that by about 10x, which should land ordinary days near 300-500/day —
  // so 950 is 2-3x headroom, not a squeeze. If a week of real traffic lands
  // higher than that, raise this rather than leaving the cap to bite: a 402
  // still stops the day by design, and the cap tripping is meant to be rare.
  "vars": { "ADSB_SOURCE": "community", "FR24_DAILY_BUDGET": "950" },

  // ── CRON REMOVED 2026-09-10 ─────────────────────────────────────────────
  // It used to read "17 5,17 * * *" — 05:17 and 17:17 UTC daily. Its ONLY job
  // was AeroDataBox work: top up flight-alert credits by BUYING them out of
  // the plan's API units (1:1), and check webhook subscription status.
  //
  // Decision, 2026-09-10: RapidAPI is not an approved provider, on cost, and
  // this spend was never authorised.
  //
  // So this fired twice a day, unattended, spending on an unapproved provider,
  // and is the largest identified consumer of the monthly quota that later ran
  // out and took aircraft type + registration off every board with it. It was
  // added by a Claude session (49665d92, 2026-08-26) on the strength of the
  // owner approving USE of that service the day before — which is not
  // authorisation to BUY from it.
  //
  // `scheduled()` in fids-proxy.js now returns immediately as well; the trigger
  // is removed here so Cloudflare does not invoke it at all. tests/
  // provider-ban.test.js fails if either protection is undone.
  //
  // DO NOT restore this trigger for RapidAPI. If flight-alert webhooks are
  // wanted again, rebuild them on an approved provider (Flightradar24) with an
  // agreed spend cap first. See docs/FLIGHT-DATA-PROVIDERS.md.
  // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  "observability": { "enabled": true }
}
